From cd4aeecd189d9b1b6aee93fa7738ddfb820dff98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 18:42:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 014/206] feat(shorebird): 256-align dispatch table offsets --
 now byte-identical

Their print_dispatch_table_link_info_to emitted offsets that are all multiples
of 256 where ours were RowFitter's compact values. This was recorded as one of
the "three differences that require the two VMs to be identical", but it turned
out to be an ordinary algorithm change, not an emergent property.

Evidence it is only the fitter that differs: the 21 selectors that land on
kOriginElement (4096) were already the exact same set in both binaries, so
pass 1 and all three sorts match upstream unmodified. Restricting the
candidate offsets in FitAndAllocate to a 256 grid is the whole change.

Verified against their own gen_snapshot on six fixtures -- hello plus the five
differential variants -- regenerating both sides from a freshly compiled dill
rather than comparing stale captures:

  hello b_shift c_body d_sig e_minimal f_heavy  ->  6/6 byte-identical
  (e_minimal is 3182 B vs 3214 B for the rest, so the content genuinely
   differs between fixtures and still matches)

The _debug_ JSON twin is byte-identical too. Closes difference #3 and takes
print_dispatch_table_link_info_to from "ranges exact, offsets diverge" to
byte-identical.

Not yet verified: that a snapshot carrying the sparser table executes
correctly. It serialises to ELF without error, and offsets past
kLargestSmallOffset use the slow path upstream already implements, but no
snapshot has been run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/compiler/aot/dispatch_table_generator.cc b/runtime/vm/compiler/aot/dispatch_table_generator.cc
index 0cb4325e430..e76ddb00c26 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/compiler/aot/dispatch_table_generator.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/compiler/aot/dispatch_table_generator.cc
@@ -142,6 +142,13 @@ class SelectorRow {
 
 class RowFitter {
  public:
+  // Selector offsets are only ever handed out on this grid. Upstream packs
+  // rows as tightly as the free list allows, which makes an offset a function
+  // of every other row in the program: adding one selector shifts the rest.
+  // A patch build has to agree with its base build on where each selector
+  // lives, so density is traded for stability. Costs a larger table.
+  static constexpr int32_t kOffsetAlignment = 256;
+
   RowFitter() : first_slot_index_(0) { free_slots_.Add(Interval(0, INT_MAX)); }
 
   // Try to fit a row at the specified offset and return whether it was
@@ -274,9 +281,11 @@ void RowFitter::FitAndAllocate(SelectorRow* row,
 
   int32_t next_offset;
 
-  int32_t offset = min_offset;
+  int32_t offset = Utils::RoundUp(min_offset, kOffsetAlignment);
   while (offset <= max_offset && !TryFit(row, offset, &next_offset)) {
-    offset = next_offset;
+    // next_offset is strictly greater than offset, so rounding it up keeps the
+    // scan monotonic and the loop terminating.
+    offset = Utils::RoundUp(next_offset, kOffsetAlignment);
   }
   if (offset <= max_offset) {
     row->AllocateAt(offset);
